From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] A deadline does not take away the last thing on the screen

Patch 0067 moved the application transition's deadline from AOSP's 5 s to 45 s
and left what happens at the deadline alone. The owner said what that is worth
(2026-11-03): "你这个方案也是一个临时方案啊。你没有解决最终 45 秒到期以后到底
怎么办？你还是提供一个空白页面，那有什么用呢？" He is right. Expiry still hides
the application on its way out while the one coming in has drawn nothing, and
what is composed then is the status bar and the navigation bar over nothing --
the original defect, forty seconds later.

What the deadline is actually for. It is not a decision about the screen. While
a transition is pending, the window manager defers committed visibility, window
removal, the IME target and the priority boost (the readers of
isTransitionSet(), in DisplayContent, AppWindowContainerController,
WindowStateAnimator, AppWindowToken, WindowState and StackWindowController), so
something has to let go if an application really is wedged. Leaving the screen
empty is a side effect of letting go, not its purpose. So this patch changes the
side effect and keeps the deadline: the transition still runs, everything it
defers is still released, and the one thing that does not happen is taking away
the last picture the reader has.

Where. handleClosingApps() in WindowSurfacePlacer is the single place the
outgoing application is hidden, and therefore the only place that can know it is
about to leave the screen with nothing on it. The condition is asked once per
transition, and only after the deadline has passed: a transition that is good to
go on its own merits has, by definition, something to show. It holds when there
is at least one application opening and not one of them has either drawn
(allDrawn) or had its starting window put up (startingDisplayed).

Letting go. The hold is released -- the held applications hidden for real -- on
the first surface placement in which every application that was opening has
something on screen of its own. A first frame is one of the things that causes a
surface placement, so this is the pass that runs when the application finally
draws. If instead those applications lost their windows -- killed, or the reader
pressed Back -- the hold is dropped without hiding anything, because the
activity manager owns that screen's visibility again and hiding it here would be
this patch producing the blank screen it exists to prevent. If they neither draw
nor go away, nothing happens and the previous screen stays, which is the point;
nothing here is on a timer.

What it costs, named rather than left to be found:

  * A surface that would have been freed is kept until the release: 1072 x 1448
    x 4 = 6.2 MB, on a device with 511 MB. One at a time in the ordinary case,
    because one transition times out at a time.
  * Between the deadline and the release, the activity manager believes the
    outgoing activity is invisible while it is in fact still on the screen. It
    is not told otherwise, so it does not stop it and does not re-show it. The
    two agree again at the release, which calls updateReportedVisibilityLocked()
    as the transition would have.
  * setVisibility() is called with performLayout false. It is called from the
    top of a surface placement which is about to lay out anyway, and asking for
    another one from in there calls performSurfacePlacement() before mInLayout
    is set, which is a recursive traversal. setLayoutNeeded() is set instead.

What it does not change:

  * The deadline. 45 s for a non-keyguard transition, AOSP's 5 s for a keyguard
    one, exactly as patch 0067 left them.
  * Any transition that has not timed out. wouldLeaveTheScreenEmpty() returns
    false before it looks at anything else, so the ordinary path does not read a
    property, walk a list, or behave differently in any way.
  * A transition with nothing opening. What a closing application uncovers is
    whatever was behind it, and that is not this question.
  * Who can take input, and the not-responding deadlines. Unchanged, so a tap
    during the wait can still raise "not responding" at 5 s x 4 = 20 s (patch
    0012) -- and a screen that still shows something is more inviting to tap
    than a blank one was.

ro.pw3.hold_screen_past_deadline, with debug.pw3.hold_screen_past_deadline over
it, both default true. The debug one is writable by adb on the release image, so
the two behaviours can be compared on one device without rebuilding -- the same
arrangement as patch 0068.

How exposed this is today: not at all, which is why it is worth doing now rather
than being forced. On 2026-11-03 all four installed entries -- Settings, the
browser, Files and WeRead -- were found to get a starting window (the
`Splash Screen <package>` window in `dumpsys window windows`; Files was checked
separately with `aapt dump --values resources`, where windowDisablePreview,
0x01010267, appears 0 times in the package). An application with a starting
window satisfies the transition at about 0.7 s and never reaches the deadline.
Only an application that turns the preview off itself, or one whose starting
window type comes out NONE, can get there.

Which is also the problem with this patch, and the reason AppTransition is
touched a second time here. Nothing installed can reach the branch being added,
so nothing would run it, and a branch nobody has run is how patch 0008 carried a
false premise for twelve days. debug.pw3.app_transition_timeout_ms shortens the
non-keyguard deadline from the device; with the starting window also turned off,
a cold start walks into the branch on purpose:

    adb shell setprop debug.pw3.disable_starting_window true
    adb shell setprop debug.pw3.app_transition_timeout_ms 2000
    adb shell setprop debug.pw3.hold_screen_past_deadline false   # the old way
    adb shell setprop debug.pw3.hold_screen_past_deadline true    # this patch

Both ways on one device, one image, no rebuild -- so what is claimed here can be
compared against what it replaced rather than argued. The property is not set by
pw3.mk and nothing reads it unless somebody sets it.

Not run on the device yet when this patch was written.

